+
+# Send the welcome email from the queue
+
+## Context
+
+Every newly registered account gets a welcome email. Sending it inside the
+registration request would tie sign-up to the mail provider: a slow provider
+makes registration hang, and an unreachable one makes it fail — for a message
+that has nothing to do with whether the account was created.
+
+Registration was also the first feature in this app to need either a queue or
+a mailer, so neither was wired up.
+
+## Decision
+
+`RegisterController.store()` dispatches `SendWelcomeEmailJob` after creating
+and signing in the user, then redirects as before. The job composes and sends
+`WelcomeMail`; the request never touches a transport. A registration that is
+refused returns before the dispatch, so nothing is queued for it.
+
+Both subsystems are configured from the environment and resolved lazily, the
+way `config/database.ts` resolves its filename — the value is read when the
+manager is first built, not when the module is imported:
+
+- `config/queue.ts` maps `QUEUE_CONNECTION` to a driver: `sync` (development,
+  runs inline), `memory` (tests, holds jobs), `redis` (production, drained by
+  `bunx guren queue:work`). `QueueProvider` binds the manager, resolves the
+  default driver — which installs it as the global one `Job.dispatch()` pushes
+  to — and registers the app's job classes so a worker can resolve them by name.
+- `config/mail.ts` maps `MAIL_MAILER` to a transport: `log` (development,
+  prints the message), `memory` (tests, records it), `resend` (real
+  delivery), with `MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS` / `MAIL_FROM_NAME` as the sender.
+  `MailProvider` binds the manager and publishes it globally, which is where
+  jobs — running outside any request container — look it up.
+
+No dispatcher names a driver or a transport, so moving from `sync`/`log` to
+`redis`/a real provider is an environment change.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Sign-up is decoupled from mail delivery: a failing send is retried by the
+queue (three attempts, exponential backoff) instead of surfacing as a failed
+registration, and the account exists either way. In exchange, production needs
+a worker process running alongside the app — with `QUEUE_CONNECTION=redis` and
+no worker, welcome emails pile up in Redis unsent.
+
+The subject and greeting name the app from `APP_NAME` (`config/app.ts`'s
+`appName()`), so a rename does not need a code change either.
